MEMORANDUM
TO:

Governing Body, City of Cheyenne
FROM:

Renee Smith, Grants Manager
DATE:

SEPTEMBER 19, 2022
SUBJECT:
RESOLUTION TO ACCEPT FEMA BRIC GRANT – Clear Creek

The City of Cheyenne was awarded a U.S. Department of Homeland Security Federal Emergency
Management Agency FY 2021 Building Resilient Infrastructure Communities grant in the amount of
$262,500.
Grant funds will be used to complete project scoping design work for the Cheyenne Clear Creek
Detention Storage Basin.  This project scoping effort/design will evaluate and identify a feasible,
constructible inline storage facility within the corporate limits of Cheyenne to realign the Ames Avenue
constriction and add storage to reduce the flood risk for residential and commercial properties.
This project’s scope-of-work will include the following tasks: Field surveys and reconnaissance for
collection and evaluation of pertinent data needed for hydraulic modeling of design alternatives including
conveyance pipes for a realignment of the Clear Creek confluence with Crow Creek, the evaluation/design
of a detention storage facility to provide flood relief for both the Ames Avenue underpass and downstream
residential properties located in the historic flow path of Clear Creek that has been inundated with larger
flood events, and digital topographic data development for use in the hydraulic and geotechnical modeling
efforts. The project also includes the development a working hydraulic model to evaluate and design
alternative storage areas and conveyance pipes using a reach specific model.
The total project cost is $350,000, designating $87,500 matching funds from the 1% Drainage Fund.
Grant Narrative: This area of our community is inundated with flood waters on a nearly annual basis causing
property damage and impacting our business owners. The Ames underpass is one of only three routes across
the Union Pacific rail yard and is critical for emergency response. Both the 1988 Dry Creek Drainage Master
Plan and the 2000 Surface Water Drainage Committee Final Report listed the UPRR Ames underpass as a
critical flood hazard. Our project scoping/design efforts for the Clear Creek storage flood control project
will lead to an application-ready mitigation project for a future BRIC funding opportunity in FY2023 and
represents a worthwhile investment of BRIC funding as we are addressing multiple community lifelines
with this project.
If you have any questions or need further clarification, please give me a call at 637-6285 or email at
rsmith@cheyennecity.org
```

### Page 2 of 11

```text
RESOLUTION NO. __________

ENTITLED: “A RESOLUTION AUTHORIZING THE ACCEPTANCE OF A FY21
BUILDING RESILIENT INFRASTRUCTURE AND COMMUNITIES
GRANT FOR SCOPING AND DESIGN WORK FOR THE CHEYENNE
CLEAR CREEK DETENTION STORAGE FACILITY.”

WHEREAS, the governing body for the City of Cheyenne desires to accept a grant from
the U.S. Department of Homeland Security (DHS) Federal Emergency Management Agency
(FEMA) under the Capability and Capacity Building Activity program; and

WHEREAS, the governing body for the City of Cheyenne recognizes the need to address
critical flood hazards; and

WHEREAS, this Project will result in an application ready construction project for future
funding opportunities; and

WHEREAS, the governing body for the City of Cheyenne accepts a grant amount of
$262,500 toward the cost of the proposed Project; and

WHEREAS, the governing body for the City of Cheyenne agrees to provide $87,500
from the 1% Drainage Fund for the required local match; and

NOW, THEREFORE, BE IT RESOLVED BY THE GOVERNING BODY OF THE
CITY OF CHEYENNE, WYOMING, that a grant contract for $262,500 be accepted from the
U.S. Department of Homeland Security Federal Emergency Management Agency for the
Cheyenne Clear Creek Detention Storage Facility scoping and design project.

PRESENTED, READ AND ADOPTED this _______ day of ________________, 2022.

__________________________________________

Patrick Collins, Mayor
(SEAL)

ATTEST:
____________________________________
Kristina F. Jones, City Clerk
By Stefanie Boster at 11:31 am, Sep 16, 2022
